# systemd unit for `vike-strategy-builder` — the ONLY process in this workspace that runs `cargo`
# against source an authenticated caller supplies. See
# `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs` (the wire protocol, the auth domain, the scope
# argument — split out of `vike-strategy-plugin` by a controller ruling so a compiled plugin never
# inherits this service's dependency tree; that module's doc carries the full argument) and
# `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-21-runtime-loaded-rust-strategies-design.md`
# ("The builder service") for what this daemon does and why it is a SEPARATE service from
# `vike-backend backtest`, which only `dlopen`s this daemon's finished `.so` files off a shared
# filesystem — the two never talk to each other over a socket (see that design's "The builder
# gets its own authenticated loopback port" section).
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example,
# not a requirement — substitute your own root everywhere, the same recipe every other unit in
# this directory documents:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-strategy-builder.service > /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service    # must print NOTHING
#   install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service ~/.config/systemd/user/vike-strategy-builder.service
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now vike-strategy-builder
#
# (For a system-wide install use /etc/systemd/system/, add `User=`, and drop the --user flags.
# Read the hardening block at the bottom first — under a --user manager the sandbox directives are
# applied as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box, exactly as `deploy/vike-backtest.service`'s own header
# warns.)
#
# ⚠ A SECOND, INDEPENDENT placeholder below — /checkout/vike-trader-rust — is NOT a sub-path of
# the project root above, and deliberately shares NO PREFIX with it: /srv/vike-<unit> is
# a prefix of a careless "/srv/vike-<unit>"-style placeholder, so the substitution
# above would rewrite this one to `$R-checkout` instead of leaving it a separate, independent
# substitution target. This placeholder is a tree holding crates/vike-model, crates/vike-strategy,
# crates/vike-indicators and crates/vike-strategy-plugin (plus the root Cargo.toml, for their
# `field.workspace = true` inheritance) — the vike-strategy-plugin cdylib template's own dependency
# closure — which a rendered plugin's Cargo.toml needs to resolve its `path` dependencies against.
#
# ⚠ **This does NOT have to be a second git checkout an operator maintains by hand, and until
# 2026-09-22 the only documented way to fill it was exactly that.** The workspace source ships as
# its own release asset now (`vike-strategy-source.tar.gz` — see
# `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s "Package the strategy builder's workspace source" step for the
# derived-closure argument and the size measurement behind shipping a subtree rather than the whole
# checkout), so the version this points at is pinned to the SAME release as the `bin/vike-backend`
# beside it, by construction rather than by an operator remembering to re-sync a checkout on every
# deploy. `scripts/fetch_release_tools.sh strategy-source [--project <root>]` downloads, verifies
# against `SHA256SUMS` and extracts it to `<project>/vendor/vike-strategy-source` — which is exactly
# the leaf this variable should then name. A real, hand-maintained checkout still works (cargo does
# not care how the tree arrived, only that `crates/vike-model` etc. are really there) and is a
# reasonable choice on a box that already keeps one for other reasons; the release asset is the
# path that keeps the two in step with no reason to remember.
#
# Include it in the SAME substitution pass, with its own `$CHECKOUT` — order does not matter now
# that the two placeholders share no prefix:
#
#   CHECKOUT=/path/to/your/vike-trader-rust     # or: <project root>/vendor/vike-strategy-source
#   sed "s,/checkout/vike-trader-rust,$CHECKOUT,g" ... (chain onto the sed above)
#   grep -n /checkout/vike-trader-rust /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service    # must print NOTHING
#
# ⚠ A THIRD placeholder — /toolchain/rust — is the Rust TOOLCHAIN this daemon compiles with, and it
# too deliberately shares no prefix with either of the two above. It names the directory holding a
# REAL `cargo` and a REAL `rustc`, not the rustup shims in `~/.cargo/bin`; that distinction is the
# whole fix for a defect measured on the CI box and is argued at the `CARGO=`/`RUSTC=` block below.
# Read the value off whichever toolchain you want this service to build plugins with:
#
#   TOOLBIN=$(dirname "$(rustup which cargo)")   # e.g. ~/.rustup/toolchains/stable-…-gnu/bin
#   sed "s,/toolchain/rust/bin,$TOOLBIN,g" ... (chain onto the sed above)
#   grep -n /toolchain/rust /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service    # must print NOTHING
#
# ⚠ THIS DAEMON SHIPS INSIDE THE MULTICALL NOW, NOT AS ITS OWN BINARY. `crates/vike/Cargo.toml`'s
# `vike-strategy-builder` feature (forwarded by `full`) carries this service as a `vike-backend
# strategy-builder` verb — the same join `trade`/`datahub`/`backtest` already went through, and for
# the identical reason: `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds `-p vike-backend --bin vike-backend
# --features full` and NOTHING ELSE, so a unit naming a separate `vike-strategy-builder` binary
# would ExecStart a file no release produces. The crate's OWN binary
# (`crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/bin/vike-strategy-builder.rs`) still exists and still builds —
# see `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs`'s `run`, which both composition roots call, for
# why folding this into the multicall does not touch `34b8d6756`'s split: that commit kept
# `tracing`/`vike-datahub-client` out of a COMPILED PLUGIN's link
# (`crates/vike-strategy-plugin`'s own tree), and this join is the DISPATCHER taking on this
# crate, which a plugin never sees either way.
#
# Building it yourself is the same one command every other daemon in this directory uses, under
# the multicall's own package — and the `--bin` follows the PACKAGE name, because
# crates/vike/Cargo.toml declares no `[[bin]]`:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full
#   install -d -m755 /srv/vike-<unit>/bin
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-backend /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend
#
# ⚠ It ALSO needs the workspace SOURCE TREE `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT` names below —
# see that variable's own note further down for where that comes from on a real deploy (a release
# asset, extracted beside the binaries — never a second git checkout an operator maintains by
# hand).
#
# ⚠ **INSTALLING THIS UNIT IS NOT THE SAME AS THIS DAEMON JOINING THE AUTOMATED DEPLOY PIPELINE —
# a SECOND, SEPARATE act is required, and it is easy to do the first and believe the second
# happened too.** `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy`'s `DEPLOY_UNITS` names this unit and carries
# a real `unit_shape` probe for it, so a green tag's automated `deploy` WILL pre-flight and restart
# it going forward — but ONLY once that script's INSTALLED copy at `/usr/local/sbin/` is refreshed.
# That script is installed OUT OF BAND, by hand (`docs/ops/upgrading.md`'s install step has the
# exact command), and nothing on this box does that for you. Until you run it, a green automated
# deploy keeps installing a fresh `bin/vike-backend` while never pre-flighting or restarting THIS
# unit — silently, because the installed helper's OLD `DEPLOY_UNITS` simply does not name it, and
# nothing on the box reports that as a problem (`verify-tools`/`helper_identity` catch a stale copy
# missing a TOOL or a VERB, not a stale `DEPLOY_UNITS` roster). Same shape as the incident that
# motivated `verify-tools` in the first place, applied to a roster instead of a tool table.
#
# ⚠ THE KEY IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE. A tracked unit is the worst place to put a real
# secret, and `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY` is the ONE credential this service refuses to start
# without (`crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs`'s `run`, called by both this crate's own
# binary and the multicall's `strategy-builder` verb). This binary does not yet route through
# this workspace's credential store (`<project>/settings/secrets.env`) the way every other daemon
# in this directory does — see that module's own doc for the scope note — so until that
# wiring lands, an operator supplies the key with a systemd drop-in:
#   systemctl edit vike-strategy-builder
#   # [Service]
#   # Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY=<a real key>
# NEVER via `EnvironmentFile=<root>/.env` below: that file is operator TUNABLES, never a
# credential source, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `no_unit_makes_the_credential_store_an_environment_source` holds every unit in this directory to
# that rule already.
#
# ⚠ `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT` is NOT a secret (it names a directory, not a
# credential), so it DOES ship below as an ordinary `Environment=` line — unlike the key, an
# operator who forgets to substitute the checkout placeholder gets an immediate, loud refusal to
# start (`builder.rs`'s `run`) rather than a silently-wrong build tree. See
# `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/render.rs`'s module doc for why there is no compile-time
# fallback here at all: a binary built in one checkout and deployed into another would otherwise
# silently point `cargo` at a tree that does not exist on this box.

[Unit]
Description=vike-strategy-builder — compiles an authenticated caller's Rust strategy into a cdylib
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

# WorkingDirectory IS the project root, the same convention every unit in this directory follows.
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
# ⚠ The multicall verb, NOT the standalone binary — see the header note above. `strategy-builder`
# is `crates/vike/src/main.rs`'s `TOOLS` row name; it takes no argv (every knob below is an
# `Environment=` line), so there is nothing to append after it.
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend strategy-builder

# --- the listen address ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Loopback is not a configuration this binary can be talked OUT of — `builder::bind_addr` fixes
# the IP half in code, so unlike its sibling daemons there is no public-bind escape hatch to guard
# against here. Only the PORT is a real knob (avoiding a collision with vike-tradehub's 7879 or
# vike-backtest's 7880 on a box that runs more than one of these).
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_PORT=7881

# --- where finished artifacts land, and how many this daemon keeps per strategy --------------
# ⚠⚠ **THIS DIRECTORY IS THE ENTIRE HAND-OFF, AND THE OTHER HALF OF IT IS IN
# `deploy/vike-backtest.service`.** The two daemons never talk over a socket; the compute daemon
# `dlopen`s the `.so` this one writes, and it finds that file by resolving
# `<its own WorkingDirectory>/user_data/plugins` — `crates/vike-studio-core/src/run.rs`'s
# `plugins_dir`, over `crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs`'s `user_plugins_dir`. It reads NO
# variable for it. So the leaf below is not a preference: it is the half of a contract whose other
# half is a code path, and a Build that returns a green sha followed by a Run that answers
# `plugin artifact not found` is what a disagreement looks like.
#
# ⚠ **SUBSTITUTE THE SAME $R INTO THIS UNIT AND INTO `deploy/vike-backtest.service`.** The two
# templates carry DIFFERENT placeholder roots (`/srv/vike-<unit>` here,
# `/srv/vike-<unit>` there) and that is not a second location — it is exactly the arrangement
# `deploy/vike-datahub.service` and `deploy/vike-backtest.service` already use for the hist store,
# and its own block there spells the rule: the pair runs on ONE box out of ONE project root, the
# `sed` at the top of each file substitutes the same `$R`, and it is the shared LEAF that makes
# the two resolve to one directory afterwards. Install this unit under a root of its OWN and the
# join is broken on that box, silently, until the first Run.
#
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `every_shipped_plugin_artifact_dir_is_one_spelling`
# holds the leaf equal on both sides — it reads THIS variable here and derives the reader's answer
# from that unit's `WorkingDirectory` there, which is the only comparison a text gate can make and
# is the one that was missing when the join first landed.
#
# ⚠ **A SPLIT ROOT IS NOT AVAILABLE IN A TRACKED UNIT, and this is where that bites.** Pointing
# this variable into another daemon's tree would need a `ReadWritePaths=` naming a path outside
# this unit's project folder, which `every_daemon_unit_is_one_project_folder` refuses — and under
# `ProtectSystem=strict` an ungranted write is `EROFS`, so the refusal is describing a physical
# fact rather than inventing a rule. One root, or an operator-owned departure this file cannot
# make for them.
#
# ⚠ **THE OWNERSHIP HAZARD, because the gate cannot see it.** `UMask=0077` at the bottom of this
# file means an artifact this daemon writes is readable by its own user and nobody else. Under a
# `--user` manager both daemons are that same user and it never bites. Under a SYSTEM install with
# a `User=` line, the two units MUST name the same account, or the compute daemon's `dlopen` fails
# with a permission error on a file that exists — which reads like a corrupt artifact and is not
# one.
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_OUT_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/user_data/plugins
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_RETAIN=5

# --- the source tree a rendered plugin's Cargo.toml resolves vike-model/vike-strategy/
# vike-indicators/vike-strategy-plugin against — REQUIRED, see the header note. NOT a sub-path of
# the project root above (a placeholder of its own, on purpose). Typically
# `<project root>/vendor/vike-strategy-source` — `scripts/fetch_release_tools.sh strategy-source`'s
# extraction target — once the project root above is substituted; a hand-maintained checkout works
# too, see the header note.
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT=/checkout/vike-trader-rust

# --- cargo's own home, RELOCATED INTO THE WRITE GRANT ----------------------------------------
# ⚠ **Without this line no build on this unit can succeed — not "a build that needs to fetch a
# crate", EVERY build.** `ProtectHome=read-only` below makes the operator's `~/.cargo` readable
# and not writable, and cargo takes an EXCLUSIVE file lock on `$CARGO_HOME/.package-cache` before
# essentially any dependency resolution. `render::build_plugin` renders a scratch crate that has
# no `Cargo.lock` at all (it is content-addressed per source sha, created fresh) and passes
# neither `--offline` nor `--locked`, so every new sha resolves `toml` plus the whole `path`
# dependency closure and therefore takes that lock. Opening a lock file for writing on a
# read-only mount is `EROFS`, and cargo fails the build. The unit's comment below used to say a
# write was needed only when "fetching a crate version this box's registry cache does not already
# hold"; that understated it by the difference between a cache miss and every single build.
#
# ⚠ **`--offline`/`--locked` were considered instead and do NOT fix it.** `--locked` requires a
# `Cargo.lock` the scratch crate does not have, so it turns every build into an error. `--offline`
# only stops cargo reaching the NETWORK — it still takes the package-cache lock, so a read-only
# `$CARGO_HOME` fails exactly the same way. A writable `$CARGO_HOME` is the fix; `--offline` is at
# most a later hardening on top of one, not a substitute.
#
# It lives under `settings/state`, which the ReadWritePaths= line below already grants, so the
# project stays one self-contained folder. The registry cache inside it is this daemon's own — an
# operator who wants the first build warm rather than cold seeds it once with
# `cp -a ~/.cargo/. <root>/settings/state/cargo-home/` before enabling the unit.
#
# ⚠ **`RUSTUP_HOME` was "reasoned, not measured" here, and the reasoning was WRONG.** It used to say
# that running an installed toolchain only READS that directory, so `ProtectHome=read-only` was
# enough. MEASURED on the CI box 2026-09-22 (systemd 255, `systemd-run --wait --pipe --collect` through
# the SYSTEM manager carrying this `[Service]` section's exact options as `-p` flags): this unit
# declares no `User=`, so a system install runs it as ROOT, `$HOME` is `/root`, and the rustup SHIM
# refuses before cargo starts at all —
#
#   error: could not create home directory: '/root/.rustup': Read-only file system (os error 30)
#
# — on EVERY build, not on the first only, and no amount of cache warming reaches it because the
# refusal happens in the shim's own startup. Two fixes were measured and BOTH build: point
# `RUSTUP_HOME` at a rustup home the unit may read, or stop going through rustup at all. This unit
# takes the second; the block immediately below argues why.
Environment=CARGO_HOME=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/cargo-home

# --- the toolchain, NAMED OUTRIGHT rather than found through rustup ---------------------------
# ⚠ **These two lines are why the `RUSTUP_HOME` problem above does not exist for this unit.** The
# other measured fix — `Environment=RUSTUP_HOME=<the operator's ~/.rustup>` plus a `PATH=` putting
# `~/.cargo/bin` first — also built cleanly (same box, same harness, same day) and is rejected for
# three reasons, none of them taste:
#   1. It makes the unit override PATH wholesale, and PATH is also how cargo finds the LINKER. A
#      unit that sets PATH to reach a compiler and quietly drops `cc` fails at the link step, which
#      surfaces as somebody else's error message.
#   2. It keeps a WRITE question alive. rustup's shim wants a home DIRECTORY; that pointing it at a
#      read-only one works is a property of rustup's current behaviour, not a guarantee — and the
#      previous version of this file already lost one bet of exactly that shape.
#   3. `~/.cargo/bin` is the directory `CARGO_HOME=` above deliberately relocates AWAY from, so the
#      unit would be naming the operator's cargo home for the shims and its own for the cache.
# Naming the toolchain's own `cargo` and `rustc` needs no `RUSTUP_HOME`, no PATH override, and no
# rustup on the box at all — and it PINS the compiler a plugin is built with, which is not nothing
# when `deploy/vike-backtest.service` `dlopen`s the result into a running process.
# `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/bin/vike-strategy-builder.rs`'s `main` reads `CARGO` out of the
# one environment sweep it owns (falling back to `"cargo"` off PATH — which, under a systemd unit's
# default PATH, is nothing at all); `RUSTC` is read by cargo itself.
# ⚠ The toolchain must be READABLE by whatever account this unit runs as, and `ProtectHome=read-only`
# below is what makes one living under a home directory reachable — see that directive's own note.
Environment=CARGO=/toolchain/rust/bin/cargo
Environment=RUSTC=/toolchain/rust/bin/rustc

# ⚠ Set for structural consistency with every other unit in this directory
# (`crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `every_daemon_unit_is_one_project_folder`
# requires every daemon to declare this), even though `vike-strategy-builder` does not yet read
# it — see this unit's header note on the credential-store gap. Harmless unread today; load-
# bearing the day this binary's settings/credential loading is wired up.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# Operator tunables (NOT credentials — see the header note above) from a plain env file at the
# PROJECT ROOT; harmless if absent.
EnvironmentFile=-/srv/vike-<unit>/.env

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

# --- the write grant -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO targets on ONE line: the settings state directory (future-proofing, alongside the
# VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR note above) and the artifact directory this daemon exists to fill. Neither
# reaches `bin/` (the binary this unit runs) or `settings/` itself (the future credential store).
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state /srv/vike-<unit>/user_data/plugins

# --- syscall / capability hardening: the SAME measured set every other daemon unit here carries -
NoNewPrivileges=true
ProtectSystem=strict
# ⚠ **THE ONE DELIBERATE DEPARTURE from every sibling unit in this directory, and the whole
# reason this service exists as a SEPARATE process rather than a feature of `vike-backtest`.**
# `ProtectHome=yes` (every other unit here) makes `/home`, `/root` and `/run/user` INACCESSIBLE —
# which is exactly what puts the Rust toolchain (`~/.rustup`, `~/.cargo`, or `$RUSTUP_HOME`/
# `$CARGO_HOME` if relocated) outside `vike-backtest.service`'s own mount namespace and is WHY
# that daemon cannot run `cargo` at all (see the design doc's "sandbox constraint" section — this
# is the same fact stated from the other daemon's side). This unit's entire job is running
# `cargo`/`rustc`, so it cannot make that same choice.
#
# `read-only` rather than dropping the directive entirely: this service can READ the operator's
# whole home directory — the toolchain, any other dotfiles living there — which
# `vike-backtest.service` and `vike-datahub.service` cannot. That is the PRICE of this process
# holding a compiler, paid once, on the one unit whose job requires it, rather than spread across
# every daemon that might otherwise have been tempted to link one in.
#
# ⚠ The SAME read grant is what makes `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT` reachable in the
# ordinary case: `ProtectSystem=strict` makes the rest of the filesystem READ-ONLY, not
# inaccessible, so the vike-trader-rust checkout stays readable from anywhere outside
# `/home`/`/root`/`/run/user` with no extra directive needed. If that checkout genuinely lives
# under the operator's home directory, `ProtectHome=read-only` above already covers it too.
#
# ⚠ **MEASURED on the CI box 2026-09-22. This said "Not measured on a real box" and named a standing
# unknown; the unknown is closed, and it was a REAL DEFECT — this unit could not complete a single
# build.** Method: a cdylib crate compiled by `systemd-run --wait --pipe --collect` through the
# SYSTEM manager with every option of this `[Service]` section passed as `-p` flags, a
# `ReadWritePaths=` over the scratch directory, and BOTH the target directory and the `$CARGO_HOME`
# wiped before every row; systemd 255. Two crates were used and they agree: a dependency-free
# cdylib, and a 17-crate closure (serde+toml) whose build makes rustc DLOPEN a proc-macro `.so` —
# the code path `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` actually governs.
#
# **The verdict: `SystemCallFilter=~@privileged` killed every build, and it is ONE syscall —
# `fchown`.** With no `User=` this unit runs as root, and cargo `fchown`s the files it creates while
# populating a COLD `$CARGO_HOME`. Denied, that is `SIGSYS`: `code=dumped/status=SYS`, ~150ms, no
# diagnostic of any kind, zero crates compiled. The `SystemCallFilter=fchown` line below re-allows
# exactly that one syscall and nothing else.
#
#   deny list                               MDWE   result (cold $CARGO_HOME, root)
#   --------------------------------------  -----  ------------------------------------
#   (no deny list)                          yes    BUILT
#   ~@privileged alone                      yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~@chown alone                           yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~fchown alone                           yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~chown / ~fchownat / ~lchown alone      yes    BUILT   (so it is `fchown` ALONE)
#   the full list below, WITHOUT the fix    yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS, 0 crates
#   the full list below, WITH the fix       yes    BUILT, 17 crates, 3/3 repeats
#
# ⚠ **TWO CONDITIONS HIDE THIS, which is how it survived review and how a re-measurement can
# "disprove" it.** (1) A WARM `$CARGO_HOME` passes: cargo only chowns while CREATING that cache, so
# the second build on a box that ever got one build through is green, and so is any probe that
# forgets to wipe it. (2) A NON-ROOT service passes: with `-p User=the operator` the as-shipped filter
# BUILT, because cargo does not chown when it is not uid 0. So the defect bites exactly the shape
# this file ships — a system unit with no `User=` — on its FIRST build, which is the only build that
# decides whether the daemon works at all.
#
# ⚠ **A handed-down bisection attributed this to `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes` and a denied
# `@resources` being fatal TOGETHER. That did not reproduce here, in either crate, cold or warm.**
# Both were varied against both crates: with `fchown` allowed, `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes` WITH
# `@resources` denied BUILT all 17 crates, and dropping either one changed no row. So NEITHER is
# given up — `@resources` still denies `setrlimit`/`sched_setaffinity`/`ioprio_set` and friends,
# `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` still denies W+X mappings, and the price of this fix is one syscall
# rather than a whole group or a whole exploit mitigation. If a future run does reproduce that pair,
# the variable to control FIRST is the one that moved every result here: whether `$CARGO_HOME` was
# cold, which is the state a fresh deployment is always in.
#
# ⚠ **The CARGO WRITE was part of that unknown and is no longer** — it is now known to be
# required on every build, and it is granted by the `CARGO_HOME` line above rather than left to
# be discovered. This comment used to say a write into `~/.cargo` was needed only when "fetching
# a crate version this box's registry cache does not already hold", i.e. on a cache MISS. That
# was wrong in the direction that matters: cargo locks `$CARGO_HOME/.package-cache` exclusively
# on essentially every resolve, and the scratch crate has no `Cargo.lock` to short-circuit one,
# so under a read-only `$CARGO_HOME` EVERY build failed — a daemon that could never do its one
# job, reported as `BuildError::Toolchain`/`BuildError::Io` to whoever asked for a plugin. The
# `~/.cargo` this directive keeps read-only is now only a SEED an operator may copy from.
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=true
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
# ⚠ …and `fchown` BACK — THE ONE DEPARTURE from the sibling units' syscall set, measured in the
# block above. `@privileged` contains `@chown` contains `fchown`, and `fchown` is the single call a
# cold cargo build makes as root. A later `SystemCallFilter=` line with no `~` re-adds to the
# allow-list, so this is a one-syscall hole in the deny above rather than a relaxation of it:
# `chown`, `lchown` and `fchownat` stay denied, and each was measured individually as NOT needed.
# ⚠ **Do NOT delete this line to "align the hardening with the other units in this directory."** No
# other unit here runs a compiler, so no other unit needs it — and that is precisely the edit that
# would put this daemon back to zero successful builds with nothing in the journal but `status=SYS`.
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `the_builder_unit_can_actually_run_cargo` exists
# to make that edit fail in CI instead of on a box.
SystemCallFilter=fchown
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
